WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.040
JOANNA SMITH: This week, we're
talking about data discovery,

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.870
planning for your
Cloud migration,

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.940
and more ways to use Kubernetes.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.760
I'm Joanna Smith, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.450
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.780
Cloud Data Catalog is now
available in public beta.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.450
Data Catalog is a fully
managed data discovery

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.610
and metadata management
service, which

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.460
means you can use Data
Catalog to easily search

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.180
for tables in Google BigQuery
or topics in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.130
You can even filter
for sensitive data

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.439
thanks to Data Catalog's
integration with Cloud Data

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:33.360
Loss Prevention.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.370
Get searching, and
see for yourself.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.980
Migration to the Cloud
unlocks some awesome benefits.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.080
But wrapping your head
around the journey there

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.268
can be daunting.

00:00:42.268 --> 00:00:44.560
To help you think through
the most important questions,

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.930
we've got an all-in-one
planning resource.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:49.630
From assessing what to move
and why, to provisioning

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.790
and configuring the new
architecture you settle on,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.430
no question is too
small for this guide.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.700
Find a quiet nook
and get started.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.950
Connecting to external
services on Kubernetes

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:03.070
has historically required
compromises, but no more

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.260
thanks to Workload Identity.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.000
Workload Identity
creates a relationship

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.490
between Kubernetes service
accounts and Cloud IAM

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.470
so that you can
define which workloads

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.980
run as which identities, grant
access to other Cloud services,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.980
and never worry about Kubernetes
secrets or IAM service account

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:18.820
keys again.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.430
Try it on GCP today.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.710
Running a database in a
distributed environment

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:24.920
can be challenging.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.120
But if inherent benefits like
rapid spin-up and repeatability

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.450
keep calling your name,
this blog is for you.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.490
In it, we'll explore
what types of databases

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.980
can be effectively run
on Kubernetes, when it

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.188
makes the most sense, and why.

00:01:38.188 --> 00:01:39.730
Make an informed
decision about where

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:43.858
to run your databases with
this fast read on the GCP blog.

00:01:43.858 --> 00:01:46.150
Now, if you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.550
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.140
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.860
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.840
And we'll see you in the Cloud.

